<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baháʼu'lláh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ḥusayn-ʻAlí, Mírzá Ḥusayn-ʻAlí Núrí, Baha'u'llah, Bahaullah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baháʼu'lláh, born Ḥusayn-ʻAlí to an aristocratic family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, was the religious leader who founded the Baháʼí Faith. At 27 he accepted the claim of the Báb and became an outspoken supporter of Bábism; imprisoned in the Síyáh-Chál dungeon, he was banished from Iran, and in 1863, in Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, he first announced his claim to a revelation from God. The Ottoman authorities held him under house arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> for four years and then in the prison-city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, and his last years were spent at the Mansion of Bahjí outside Acre. He wrote at least 1,500 letters, among them the Hidden Words, the Kitáb-i-Íqán and the Kitáb-i-Aqdas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baha-ullah.jpg" alt="Baháʼu'lláh" width="330" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: passport photo taken in Adrianople (present day-Edirne) when He was under house arrest, published in William Miller’s Baha’i Faith (1974). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bahá'u'lláh_(Mírzá_Ḥusayn-`Alí_Núrí)_in_1868.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baháʼu'lláh was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> on 12 November 1817. His father, Mírzá ʻAbbás Núrí, known as Mírzá Buzurg, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath-Ali Shah Qajar</a>, and as the son of a nobleman in the province of Núr he was known as Mírzá Ḥusayn-ʻAlí Núrí. In 1835, aged eighteen, he married Ásíyih Khánum, then fifteen. In his early twenties he declined the life of privilege that his lineage offered and devoted time and money to charity, which earned him renown as the Father of the Poor. In 1844 the Báb, a young merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, claimed to be the promised redeemer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and a new prophet, and Baháʼu'lláh accepted his claim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 15 August 1852 two Bábí youths tried to assassinate the king, Nasiri'd-Din Shah, in retaliation for the killing of the Báb, and persecution of the Bábís followed. Baháʼu'lláh was imprisoned in the Síyáh-Chál, where, he said, he received his mission as a manifestation of God. Proved innocent of the attempt, he was freed but banished from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and in January 1853 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. In April 1854 he left for Iraqi Kurdistan, saying he wished not to cause disagreement among the Bábís, and returned after about seven years.</p><p>In April 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> ʻAbdu'l-ʻAzíz invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. On 22 April, in a garden across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, he declared to his companions that he was 'Him whom God shall make manifest'. He reached Constantinople on 16 August 1863 and Adrianople on 12 December. In July 1868 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> condemned him and his family to perpetual imprisonment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, while his half-brother Mirza Yahya, who had challenged him, was sent to Famagusta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Baháʼu'lláh taught a single uncreated God and held that successive manifestations of God, such as the Buddha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and Muhammad, gradually educate humanity. He urged the oneness of mankind, the removal of religious and racial prejudice, the equality of women and men and universal education, and he forbade religious violence, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. His writings, mostly in Persian and Arabic, amount to more than 100 volumes, among them the Hidden Words, the Kitáb-i-Íqán and the Kitáb-i-Aqdas. Before his death in 1892 he wrote the Book of My Covenant, naming his eldest son ʻAbdu'l-Bahá as successor. Baháʼís now live in 236 countries and territories and number between 5 and 8 million.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baháʼu'lláh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
